    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_7a1a8467c917c93d2a95ffd6c8de3eea117143ce7f06f17b69c16b2f94e666d9",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "This passage uses the known eighth-moment scale as a conditional optimality stress test for Conjecture 3, not as a derivation of it. After setting m=4 and k=l=2, the heuristic selects the relevant contribution coming from the discrete spectrum in Z₂¹(s); repeated integration by parts frequency-matches its terms through |t+κ_j|≤t^ε. Conjectural truncation, the asymptotics of R₁, division into unit-width spectral blocks, interchange, and supremum extraction then route the global moment mass toward a large short oscillatory Hecke sum. Conceptually, if every selected block were substantially below the proposed square-root scale, this representation could not account for the starting moment mass under those reductions. The burden remains on the conjectural implication and truncation, the isolation and interchange steps, and the formula-sensitive final estimate, so the passage supports only heuristic essential optimality.",
