    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_f2d84821aa7cf49b4279c3cf3890ec0e31743cce010af00de6d1c1b0ba1691ce",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The two lemmas provide asymmetric, not cleanly bidirectional, mean-square transfer. Lemma 4 sends a weighted source L² bound to a truncated modified-Mellin-transform mean square. In the converse-style direction, a smooth dyadic cutoff controls endpoints, polar residue extraction separates a nonoscillatory polynomial contribution, and contour displacement plus repeated integration by parts restricts the relevant frequencies before Cauchy–Schwarz and Lemma 4 are applied. The resulting source mean-square bound still contains an additive local source/polar term proportional to a logarithmic factor times a local integral of |f|; it is therefore not determined by the transform mean square alone. Using the bridge requires the stated smoothness and derivative-integral bounds on f, analytic continuation with prescribed pole structure, and polynomial vertical growth of F*, and the damaged extraction does not support reconstructing exact exponents.",
